# The GUI floor (01-boot-layer.md §2), as direct dependencies — apt resolves the closure,
# and `--apt-recommends false` keeps it from widening.
#
# This is the irreducible floor for any GUI on bare KMS, not Slint's overhead: libinput,
# libxkbcommon, freetype, fontconfig, fonts-dejavu and their closure. The competing
# measurement was Tauri at 268 packages / 650 MiB (docs/research/05-tauri-viability.md).
#
# **The count and size are an owed measurement** (00-overview.md): the 22 packages / 21 MiB
# measured for ADR-0002 included libseat1 and seatd, which this image no longer ships. Do
# not restate the old number here as though it still held.
#
# The published package manifest (ADR-0012) is what makes this auditable without
# building the image.

# Slint on backend-linuxkms-noseat + renderer-software. No X server, no Wayland
# compositor, no cage, no display manager, no seat daemon, no GPU driver.
#
# xkb-data arrives transitively — libxkbcommon0 depends on it — which is why the pinned
# us keymap (#54, the unit's XKB_DEFAULT_* lines) costs no package here.
libinput10
libxkbcommon0
libfontconfig1
libfreetype6
fonts-dejavu-core

# Deliberately absent, each for a reason recorded in the spec:
#
#   seatd, libseat1   §2, ADR-0016 — libseat is what stopped /dev/fb0 from being opened
#                          at all: under the seatd backend the open is delegated to a
#                          daemon that hands back DRM and evdev nodes only. -noseat makes
#                          it a plain open(2), and LIBSEAT_BACKEND is not set anywhere
#   amdgpu, xe,       §3, ADR-0016 — deleted from the module tree and the initramfs by
#   radeon (modules)       0200-aobs-no-network-modules; firmware-less they take the
#                          framebuffer aperture and then fail
#   network-manager   §3 — 7.6 MiB, and worthless as a guarantee on its own
#   firmware-*        §3 — no network driver gets firmware because none is installed
#   udisks2, gvfs     §4 — nothing exists to mount anything
#   kdump-tools       §4 — no kernel crash dumps
#   v4l-utils         §7 — the app talks V4L2 ioctls directly
#   libv4l-0t64       §7 — same
#   any terminal emulator, SSH server or getty
#                     §2 — nothing here is meant to be driven from a shell, so one is
#                          only a mistake surface, and it advertises a capability the
#                          appliance does not offer
